Robert M. Riches Jr. <rob@gtedhcp-60.inetarena.com> wrote:
> On 30 Sep 2001 08:16:36 -0700, Richc <richc5141@yahoo.com> wrote:
>>I have been searching the posts in the news groups for information on
>>em86 for alpha-linux.  It appears that this is dead and gone.  Is this
>>true?

> Not that this is a definitive answer, but I don't see an em86 RPM
> of any kind on the RH7.1 CDs.  That's not a good sign.

em86 requires very specific versions of glibc, both on Alpha and x86
side, to work with.  Nothing actually prevents you from installing these
versions side by side with your "regular" libraries (I believe that
'compat' package from 6.2 carries correct versions and that would make
that easy) and switching enviroment to load _those_ libraries if you are
so inclined.  Not that I ever had a good reason to try that but most
likely it would work with a judicious use of LD_LIBRARY_PATH and/or
LD_PRELOAD.  Shell "wrappers" for your x86 programs should do the job.
This does not guarantee that your programs will really work.  The
whole thing was always somewhat "wobbly" (signals in particular).

It goes without saying that you need to run a kernel with em86 support
before trying anything in this direction.

> I too would like to see a working X86 emulator, but writing one is
> quite a bit beyond my capabilities.

Writing that required not only capabilities but also an "insider
information" and access, and permission to use, binary-only proprietary
piece of code which was doing an ultimate translation.  Once Jim
Paradis, who was the author of em86 - I believe, got busy with other
things there was nobody who could anything about that.  Also, IIRC, Jim
left DEC shortly after it was bought by Compaq.

As opposed to FX!32 translator for NT, which was using the same
technology, em86 never stored a translated code for later use and
this means that it was slow - at least on a startup.  The most widely
used with it piece of software from x86 was Netscape.  Once Compaq
made available DU libraries (Tru64-compat package) required to run
DU Netscape binaries under Linux an interest in em86 mostly waned.

You still should be able to find em86 rpms on ftp.alphalinux.org.
I would not include that in _any_ distribution.  No way for a distro
maker to test it, fix it or support.  You want to hack it for a private
enjoyment - go ahead!

  Michal


