Hello all,

Yes, I've finally managed to install Red Hat 7.1 on to my Alpha 5/300
Noritake Alphaserver 1000a box with SRM.

I'll detail now the trials and tribulations of getting this beast to work.

Firstly, thankyou to all who suggested this and that to make it work. All
suggestions were tried, with varying degrees of success, but as to the _root
cause_ of continual installation failures... read on!

Well, I bought this machine at an auction of computer hardware. It was
marked as "Alphaserver 1000a 5/300 - Seven 4.3Gb HDDs, unknown RAM, power
supply dead."

I bid on it, not caring if the power supply worked or not - I was interested
in the disks.

Got it home and set it up on the bench. Yup, power supply's dead. Further
investigation revealed a kill switch if the top lid's removed, and the power
switch on the front was not connected. Fixed both the kill switch and the
connector, and all was well.

Next step was to get the user's guide and tech reference. Quick zap over to
Compaq and I had the manuals. Studying them revealed the RAM installed in
the machine was improperly installed, and in the wrong slots. The machine
appeared to have been pillaged for it's RAM, so I had to scrabble around to
find four RAM modules that worked. Good. Now I've got a machine that boots,
with 128Mb of matched RAM installed.

Next, download ISOs of distributions that supported Alpha. I got two;
Debian, and Red Hat.

This is where things get interesting.

Because I didn't know how to switch between SRM and ARC consoles, I was
hopelessly trying to install Red Hat using an ARC console. More reading of
the Red Hat manual indicated it didn't support this. On an Alpha box that
seemed to only run AlphaBIOS, that seemed fatal.

More reading revealed the F6/Advanced CMOS setup option to switch between
ARC and SRM. Eureka! Now we're in business!

Ok, we're booting with SRM, and can now boot the Red Hat CD using "boot
dkb400 -fl 0" - dkb400 being the CDROM on the add-in Sybios SCSI controller.

Red Had begins to run the installer, but crashes with "you may now reboot
the machine", after detecting the mouse type.

Problem Number 1: Red Hat installer does not detect a serial mouse plugged
in to /dev/ttyS0 if a PS/2 mouse is not installed.

So I removed the PS/2 mouse, and plugged in a serial one (not detected), and
the installer defaulted back to text mode installation.

The text mode installer proceeded well, but could not detect the integrated
video chip.

Problem Number 2: Red Hat installer doesn't detect the on-board Cirrus Logic
CL-5429 video chip.

I manually selected the "Cirrus Logic CL-542x" chipset and continued with
the installation.

To my horror, I found my CD 2 of Red Hat was corrupt.

Problem Number 3: There is NO WAY to either Skip, or Abort a corrupt RPM
file in the text mode installer. The only option is to press the reset
button.

Several installs later, I found that three RPM files were corrupt:

xpf-0.92-4.alpha.rpm
xfree86-devel-4.0.3-21.alpha.rpm
zlib-devel-1.1.3-22.alpha.rpm

After I found which RPM files were corrupt, I manually deselected them from
the installer.

Good, now the installer gets all the way to the end, and finalised the
installation.

Problem Number 4: The installer does not cleanly reboot the machine. All I
get is scrolling hex code ripping up the screen. I have to press the reboot
button.

With great anticipation, I waited for the machine to restart.

Nope, no good. After all that time, the machine reports no valid boot loader
found. CTRL-C puts me back at the SRM prompt.

Problem number 5: The text mode installer appears not to be able to install
a valid boot loader, or does it fail because I removed those packages and
their dependencies?

Now that I know why the GUI mode wouldn't start up, I disabled the onboard
video chip, and installed a cheapy S3 Trio 64 PCI video card.

Started up the machine, CTRL-C'd the boot sequence, and restarted the Red
Hat install from the CD.

(By now I'd downloaded another copy of the Seawolf Alpha ISO CD 2 image and
burned it).

Red Hat now quite happily started the GUI mode installer, installed all
default packages for a workstation from both CD 1 and CD 2, installed a boot
loader correctly, but still failed to cleanly reboot the machine.

Rebooted the machine using the button, and presto! The machine booted Red
Hat Alpha Linux in to the KDE desktop.

I found after all that, I didn't need the Alpha Jumpstart at all. Red Hat &
Debian install quite well without it, as long as your hardware is supported.

That was the biggest bug: Because Red Hat & Debian couldn't start a GUI (X
Free?) installer, they crashed with very cryptic messages. Wouldn't it be
better if, when the hardware probe could not detect a valid video device, it
defaults to text with a warning that the video card type was not detected?
Hmmm....

I also found that if I removed ALL BAR ONE SCSI hard disks (/dev/sda) the
installer worked quite well. If multiple hard disks are installed, the
installer sprays partitions and programs all over the place! It also seemed
to fail to install a boot loader correctly.

By the way, does any one else have a Alphaserver with seven hard disks in
it? Do your hard disks run SO HOT YOU CAN'T TOUCH THEM???!?!?! The must be
at around 70-80 degrees C! That is a VERY POOR design arrangement by
Digital. No air cooling, and the disks themselves are encased in quite tight
fitting plastic shells. Jesus, they were hot!

My comments here are not written to pick on Red Hat specifically, or Debian.
Similar problems were experienced by both distributions, due almost entirely
to the onboard video chip being unsupported or, at least, not capable of
being detected on initial install. Once a supported video card was
installed, all was happy. Hopefully the right people at Red Hat and Debian
will read what's written here and look in to it, although sadly, the Alpha
might be a dying breed.

I tell you, it was a HUGE thrill to get Red Hat and Debian running properly,
and do they run like cut cats on Alpha? You betcha. I am VERY impressed with
the speed, and the desktop!

Hooray for Linux!

Regards to all, and thanks again for your emails!

Mark






